INPUT PARAGRAPH
---------------
Technology has changed the way people learn, communicate, and solve problems. In a classroom, students can use digital books, videos, and learning platforms to explore ideas beyond the textbook. A good learning platform does not replace a teacher; instead, it gives students more ways to practise, ask questions, and receive feedback. Teachers can use technology to organise lessons, share resources, and notice which topics need more attention. However, technology is useful only when people use it thoughtfully. A fast internet connection cannot guarantee understanding, and copying information from a website is not the same as learning it. Students still need to read carefully, compare evidence, discuss different opinions, and explain ideas in their own words. They also need time away from screens to think, write, and work with other people. Schools should therefore combine digital tools with conversation, practical activities, and clear guidance. When access is fair and the tools are chosen carefully, technology can support curiosity and make education more flexible. The main goal is not to use the newest device; the goal is to help every learner build knowledge, confidence, and useful skills for the future.

